// Original practice sources, not extracts from a textbook. Numeric scenarios are fictional unless labelled otherwise.
const GEO_BANK_TEXT=`
A river runs beside a town. A bridge connects homes to a factory. Trees have been cleared for a road.~Which is a physical feature?~River~Factory~Bridge~Which interaction is supported by this scene?~People changed vegetation to build transport~The river was built by the factory~All trees grow because of roads~The road replaces trees: a human decision changes the physical environment.~Observe the scene|Separate physical and human features|Connect people and environment
A field team must measure air temperature, direction and distance. A thermometer measures temperature; a compass gives direction; a tape measures short distances.~Which tool measures temperature?~Thermometer~Compass~Camera~Which tool is most suitable for measuring a classroom wall?~Tape measure~Rain gauge~Compass~Choose equipment that measures the required variable, rather than a tool that only records appearance.~Choose an enquiry|Choose suitable instruments|Record measurements
Practice enquiry: pupils counted traffic outside school for ten minutes at 08:00 and 14:00 on one Tuesday. Counts were 80 and 25.~What is the measured variable?~Number of vehicles~Quality of every road~Annual air pollution~Which conclusion stays within this evidence?~More vehicles were counted at 08:00 in this sample~Traffic is always lower in every afternoon~Cars caused every illness in the town~Two samples support a comparison of those times, but not a claim about every day.~Ask a focused question|Collect relevant evidence|Evaluate a conclusion
The Iron Bridge crosses the River Severn. It was erected in 1779. The area developed iron industries and river transport; its industrial heritage later became important for visitors.~Which river does the Iron Bridge cross?~Severn~Thames~Nile~A drawing shows factories beside the river. What extra evidence would best test a claim of industrial growth?~Factory records from different dates~One modern souvenir~The colour of the frame~Dated records allow comparison through time; a single image cannot establish every cause of change.~Compare dated sources|Identify landscape changes|Test causes against records
Command words: identify names a feature; describe gives its characteristics or pattern; explain gives reasons using linked causes and effects.~Which command asks for reasons?~Explain~Identify~List~Question: explain why a road follows a valley. Choose the strongest response.~Gentler gradients can make construction and travel easier~There is a road~The road is long~The response links a physical feature to a reason for the route rather than merely naming it.~Identify a feature|Describe its pattern|Explain a cause
Practice evidence: after trees were removed from a test plot, more sediment was collected in runoff. A neighbouring vegetated plot retained more soil. Rainfall was similar.~Which claim is supported?~The cleared plot lost more sediment in this test~All floods are caused by logging~Trees stop every drop of rain~Which reasoning best connects evidence to the claim?~Vegetation can protect soil and roots help bind it~Bare soil creates rainfall~All runoff travels uphill~The comparison supports a vegetation link under these conditions, without proving that only one factor matters.~State a claim|Select relevant evidence|Link evidence to the claim
Practice sources: a river level rose after prolonged rainfall. A road closed in the low floodplain, but homes on the hill remained accessible.~Which location was directly affected?~Low floodplain road~Every hilltop~Every road in the country~Which conclusion uses both sources?~Low-lying access was disrupted while higher homes stayed accessible~All homes flooded equally~Rainfall cannot affect roads~Use both the river evidence and the contrasting locations, and avoid extending the claim to unobserved places.~Identify source details|Link evidence from sources|State a qualified conclusion
A walking map shows paths and contours. A street plan shows named roads. A historical map records an area at an earlier date.~Which map helps investigate past land use?~Historical map~Current bus timetable~Weather symbol key~Which map is best for estimating steepness on a hike?~Contour walking map~Undated sketch without height~World political map~Map purpose matters: contour information supports slope decisions, while other maps answer different questions.~Identify the map purpose|Read its key and date|Choose it for a suitable enquiry
Practice scale: 1 cm on a plan represents 2 m on the ground. A classroom wall measures 6 cm on the plan.~What ground distance does 1 cm represent?~2 m~2 km~20 cm~How long is the classroom wall?~12 m~3 m~6 m~Multiply 6 cm by 2 m per centimetre; keep the ground units consistent.~Read the scale|Measure the plan|Convert to ground distance
Two pupils remember different routes to school. One marks shops; the other marks a park and footbridge. Neither sketch is drawn to scale.~What is this kind of remembered sketch?~Mental map~Surveyed contour map~Satellite photograph~Why might the maps differ?~People notice and use different places~One pupil must be dishonest~Every mental map has exact scale~Mental maps reflect experience and perception; compare them with observations before judging accuracy.~Recall landmarks|Compare different memories|Check against observations
An aerial view shows a river west of a school, a road south of it, and playing fields east of it. North is at the top.~Where are the playing fields?~East of the school~West of the school~North of the school~Which map would preserve the source information?~School between the river and fields~Fields west of the river~Road north of all features~A useful sketch preserves relative positions and uses a key; unnecessary detail can be omitted.~Find north|Place major features|Add symbols and a key
Read eastings first, then northings. A four-figure reference identifies a grid square by its southwest corner. Six figures locate a point more precisely within it.~Which do you read first?~Eastings~Northings~Altitude~A square has southwest corner easting 23, northing 45. What is its reference?~2345~4523~2354~Go along the corridor before up the stairs: easting precedes northing.~Read the easting|Read the northing|Combine the reference
Practice scale: 1 cm represents 0.5 km. A curved path measures 8 cm along its bends; a direct track measures 5 cm.~Which route measurement follows the bends?~8 cm~5 cm~0.5 cm~What is the curved path distance?~4 km~16 km~8 km~Measure along the route and then multiply by the scale; straight-line distance can underestimate a winding path.~Trace the route|Measure the traced length|Apply the scale
A practice walking map shows a footbridge, a steep slope and a campsite. The key explains each symbol. A direct route crosses the river where there is no bridge.~What explains a map symbol?~The key~The title alone~The paper size~Which plan best responds to the river constraint?~Use the mapped footbridge and check the extra distance~Assume the river is safe everywhere~Ignore all symbols~Symbols and route constraints matter together; the shortest straight line may not be a usable walk.~Read the key|Locate safe crossings|Check distance and slope
Contours connect points of equal height. Closely spaced contours indicate a steeper slope; wider spacing indicates a gentler slope.~What does one contour connect?~Equal heights~Equal temperatures~Equal rainfall~Which route usually has the gentler slope?~Across widely spaced contours~Across tightly packed contours~Any route with more ink~The same height change spread across more horizontal distance makes a gentler gradient.~Read contour values|Compare spacing|Infer the slope
Latitude measures north or south of the Equator. Longitude measures east or west of the prime meridian. Bishkek is approximately 43°N, 75°E.~What does latitude describe?~North or south position~Only altitude~Only east or west position~Which hemispheres contain Bishkek?~Northern and Eastern~Southern and Western~Northern and Western~The N and E labels give the hemispheres; latitude and longitude are different coordinates.~Read latitude|Read longitude|Check both hemispheres
The UK comprises England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland. Great Britain is the island containing England, Scotland and Wales. Kyrgyzstan has no ocean coastline.~Which UK country is not on Great Britain?~Northern Ireland~Wales~Scotland~Which statement correctly compares the UK and Kyrgyzstan?~The UK has coasts; Kyrgyzstan is landlocked~Both are landlocked~Kyrgyzstan has an Atlantic coast~Landlocked countries lack a sea coast and depend on cross-border routes for access to seaports.~Locate the countries|Identify sea access|Explain a transport consequence
A political map shows countries and borders. A physical map shows landforms such as mountains, plains and rivers. A river can cross a political boundary.~Which layer shows national borders?~Political~Rainfall~Contour only~Why combine political and physical maps?~To compare borders with landscape features~Because every mountain is a border~Because rivers never cross borders~The layers answer different questions and may overlap without matching exactly.~Read political boundaries|Add physical features|Compare the layers
Practice forecast: western hills show rain and 9°C; an eastern lowland station shows cloud and 13°C today. Climate describes patterns over many years.~Which is a weather observation?~Today's 9°C reading~A thirty-year rainfall average~A long-term seasonal pattern~Which forecast is supported today?~The western station is cooler and wetter~The entire UK is always rainy~The east never receives rain~A daily source supports a local forecast, not a permanent statement about climate.~Read weather symbols|Compare station data|Make a limited forecast
Practice interviews: one UK coastal resident works in fishing; an urban resident works in healthcare; a rural resident works remotely in design.~What do these interviews show?~Different jobs and experiences~One job for all UK residents~Every community is identical~Which claim fits this small sample?~The three residents have different livelihoods~All coastal people fish~Every rural person farms~Specific examples show diversity, but three interviews cannot describe the whole population.~Read each account|Compare similarities and differences|Avoid unsupported generalisations
Practice map: houses cluster around a road junction; farms are scattered on surrounding land; a ribbon of homes follows a valley road.~Which pattern follows a line?~Linear settlement~Dispersed farms~Empty land~Why might homes cluster at a road junction?~Routes can improve access to services and travel~Road junctions prevent all trade~Every junction is flood-free~Access is a plausible factor; evidence about water, slope and hazards is also needed.~Identify a pattern|Suggest a factor|Check additional evidence
Fictional districts: A has income index 80 and access-to-water index 40; B has income 60 and water access 90. Both indicators use the same 0–100 scale.~Which district has higher water access?~B~A~They are equal~Which comparison is most defensible?~A leads in income but B leads in water access~A is better in every way~Income alone proves happiness~Different indicators capture different dimensions and can produce contrasting rankings.~Choose indicators|Compare like measures|Recognise the limitations
London contains Parliament, major railway terminals, offices and museums. These places support political, transport, business and cultural functions.~Which function does Parliament represent?~Government~Crop production~Glacial erosion~How can London and Bishkek be compared fairly?~Compare equivalent city functions in both~Assume their population is identical~Use only one London landmark~Common categories allow comparisons without pretending the cities have identical sizes or histories.~Choose a function|Find evidence in each city|Compare using the same category
Fictional product route: cotton farm → fabric mill → clothing factory → port → shop. Travel, trade and communication link these stages.~Which connection moves goods for sale?~Trade~Weathering~Latitude~A port closes temporarily. What is a plausible effect?~Deliveries may be delayed or rerouted~Cotton immediately stops growing worldwide~Shops are no longer connected to factories~Disruption at one node can affect later stages, depending on stocks and alternative routes.~Cotton farm|Fabric mill|Clothing factory|Port|Shop
A valley has a broad U-shaped cross-section, scratched bedrock and a large boulder unlike nearby rock. These can be evidence of past glaciation.~Which feature is consistent with glacial erosion?~Scratched bedrock~A modern traffic sign~A painted fence~What is the strongest approach to reconstructing its past?~Use several landscape clues together~Assume every boulder proves the exact ice age date~Ignore the rock evidence~Multiple clues support an interpretation; precise timing needs additional dated evidence.~Observe landforms|Compare possible processes|Qualify the conclusion
A simplified glacier starts with 10 ice tokens. Snow accumulation adds tokens; melting removes them. Repeated surplus adds mass, while repeated deficit reduces mass.~What happens when accumulation exceeds melting?~Ice mass increases~Ice mass must decrease~Ice turns into rock~Add 6 tokens and melt 4. What is the new total?~12~8~20~Mass balance is gain minus loss; glacier length may respond more slowly than this simple token model.~Snow accumulates|Snow compacts into ice|Ice flows under gravity
Glacial abrasion occurs when debris in moving ice scrapes rock. Plucking removes blocks as ice freezes to rock and moves. Ice also transports debris.~Which process scrapes bedrock?~Abrasion~Condensation~Deposition only~A block freezes to the glacier and is pulled away. Which process is this?~Plucking~Evaporation~Solution alone~The mechanism distinguishes removal by pulling from scraping by debris.~Ice holds debris|Ice moves over rock|Debris abrades the surface
Glaciers erode hollows into corries. A narrow ridge between corries can become an arête. Several corries eroding around a summit can form a pyramidal peak.~What is a narrow ridge between corries?~Arête~Floodplain~Delta~Which explanation fits a pyramidal peak?~Several corries erode back around a summit~A river deposits sand at its mouth~Sea waves build a spit~The position and interaction of eroding corries explain the remaining sharp summit.~Corries erode back|Ridges narrow|A sharp peak remains
A glacier can widen and deepen a valley to make a U-shaped trough. A smaller tributary valley may be left above the main trough as a hanging valley.~Which cross-section fits a glacial trough?~Broad U shape~Narrow V shape only~Perfect flat circle~Why may a tributary valley hang above the main valley?~The larger glacier eroded its valley more deeply~The tributary flowed uphill~Ice always deposits a wall there~Different amounts of erosion can leave the tributary floor higher than the main trough.~Glaciers occupy valleys|Main glacier erodes more deeply|Tributary valley is left hanging
Glaciers carry mixed rock debris. When ice melts it can leave unsorted till. Lateral moraine lies along the sides; terminal moraine marks an ice-front position.~Which deposit lies at the glacier's sides?~Lateral moraine~River delta~Sand dune~What can a terminal moraine indicate?~A former ice-front position~The exact age of every rock~A permanent ocean coastline~A ridge can mark where debris accumulated near a glacier front, but dating needs other evidence.~Debris enters the ice|Ice transports debris|Melting leaves sediment
The Lake District in northwest England contains lakes, steep mountains and glacial valleys. Walking, farming and tourism use this landscape in different ways.~Which past process helped shape these valleys?~Glacial erosion~Only road building~Only volcanic ash today~A proposed path crosses wet ground. What is a reasonable management response?~Assess erosion and consider a more durable route~Assume every visitor causes no impact~Close all farming everywhere~Site evidence helps balance access with soil, habitat and landscape protection.~Read landscape evidence|Compare proposed uses|Assess environmental effects
Fictional valley: glacier melt contributes summer water. Farms, homes and ecosystems share the river. A smaller glacier can eventually provide less meltwater even if short-term melting rises.~Which users can depend on the river?~Homes, farms and ecosystems~Only tourists~Only factories~Why plan for changing meltwater?~Seasonal supply and competing needs can change~More melt guarantees more water forever~Glaciers cannot affect rivers~Short-term melt and long-term ice storage differ; plans need timing, demand and ecosystem evidence.~Track seasonal supply|Compare user needs|Plan for uncertainty
A river source is its beginning; tributaries join it at confluences; the mouth is where it enters another water body. The Thames flows through London toward the North Sea.~What is a river junction called?~Confluence~Watershed~Contour~Which sequence follows water downstream?~Source → tributary junction → mouth~Mouth → source → tributary~Ocean → summit → source~A river network directs water downstream, with tributaries adding flow along the route.~Source|Tributary junction|Lower river|Mouth
Water can evaporate, condense into cloud droplets, fall as precipitation and enter rivers through runoff or groundwater. Not every drop takes the same route.~Which process changes liquid water to vapour?~Evaporation~Precipitation~Deposition of sediment~Rain enters the soil. What is this transfer called?~Infiltration~Condensation~Glacial plucking~Infiltration moves water into the ground; it is one of several routes within the water cycle.~Evaporation|Condensation|Precipitation|Surface runoff
A simplified river profile becomes less steep downstream. Channels generally become wider and deeper as tributaries add water, although local exceptions occur.~Where is the gradient usually steepest?~Upper course~Mouth in every river~Every reach equally~Which downstream change is common?~A wider channel and greater discharge~Water flowing uphill~All sediment instantly disappears~Tributary inputs commonly increase discharge; local rock, structures and flow conditions can cause exceptions.~Upper course|Middle course|Lower course|Mouth
Rivers erode, transport and deposit material. When a river loses the ability to carry a particular sediment load, deposition can occur.~Which process leaves sediment behind?~Deposition~Abrasion~Hydraulic action~A river slows as it enters still water. What is likely for some sediment?~It may be deposited~It must all become vapour~It must travel faster~Reduced flow energy can reduce transport capacity, depending on sediment size and other conditions.~Erode material|Transport sediment|Lose transport capacity|Deposit sediment
Practice landforms: V-shaped valley, waterfall, gorge, meander, oxbow lake and floodplain. Outer meander bends tend to erode; inner bends tend to deposit.~Where is deposition common on a meander?~Inner bend~Outer bend only~Above the clouds~How can an oxbow lake form?~A meander neck is cut through and the old loop becomes isolated~A glacier carves a corrie~A volcano fills a valley~Erosion narrows a meander neck; a cutoff and deposition can separate the old loop.~Meander neck narrows|River cuts through neck|Old loop is sealed|Oxbow lake remains
Fictional river users include irrigated farms, households, anglers and a hydropower scheme. Water quantity, timing and quality affect their needs.~Which use withdraws water to grow crops?~Irrigation~Navigation only~Birdwatching only~Which agreement best considers several needs?~Set withdrawals while keeping an agreed river flow~Give all water to one user forever~Ignore water quality~An agreement must consider competing uses and the remaining flow needed by ecosystems.~Identify users|Compare needs|Negotiate a compromise
An estuary is the tidal lower part of a river where fresh water and seawater mix. The Thames Estuary supports shipping and important habitats.~Which influence distinguishes an estuary?~Tides and mixing waters~Permanent glacier cover~No connection to the sea~Why assess a port expansion carefully?~Shipping benefits can conflict with habitat and tidal conditions~Estuaries contain no living things~Tides never affect construction~Assess economic uses alongside habitats, sediment movement and tidal constraints.~Identify tidal conditions|Map uses and habitats|Assess a proposal
Fictional flood: several wet days saturated soil; heavy rain followed; paved surfaces routed water quickly to a river. Low homes and a road flooded.~Which factor reduced infiltration?~Saturated soil~Dry empty storage alone~A lower rainfall total~Which chain fits the evidence?~Heavy rain and limited infiltration → more runoff → higher river level~Paved surfaces → no runoff → no river flow~High ground → every home floods equally~Flood risk reflects rainfall, catchment conditions and exposure; impacts vary by location.~Prolonged rain saturates soil|Heavy rain increases runoff|River level rises|Exposed places flood
Fictional Thames practice file, NOT a named textbook event: river readings rose after prolonged rain; a riverside road shut; a nearby uphill school stayed open. No damage-cost figure is supplied.~Which fact is in this practice file?~A riverside road closed~Every London school closed~Damage was exactly £50 million~Which report is accurate?~The road closed, but the file gives no total damage cost~Invent a cost to complete the story~Claim the uphill school flooded~Keep supported facts separate from missing information. Replace this file with the assigned textbook case before assessing that event.~Read the evidence|Separate facts from gaps|Write a qualified report
Fictional town: river flooding threatens homes and a wetland. Defences can lower risk but have costs, maintenance needs and limits during extreme events.~Which is an example of flood management?~Flood storage area~Removing every warning system~Building blindly in the channel~Why combine different measures?~Warnings and physical measures address different parts of risk~A wall eliminates all future risk~Every measure has the same effect~Defences can reduce probability or impacts, but residual risk and environmental trade-offs remain.~Identify exposure|Choose complementary measures|Test a severe scenario
Africa is a continent crossed by the Equator and prime meridian. The Atlantic lies to its west and the Indian Ocean to its east.~Which ocean lies west of Africa?~Atlantic~Pacific~Arctic~Which statement about Africa is correct?~It spans both sides of the Equator~It is one country~It lies wholly south of the Equator~Reference lines divide hemispheres; Africa includes places north and south of the Equator.~Locate the continent|Mark reference lines|Check hemispheres
African places have varied histories including kingdoms, trade networks, colonial rule and independence. A historical event needs a date, place and source before its geographical effects can be judged.~What is needed for a reliable timeline?~Dated events and sources~Only a stereotype~One present-day photograph for every century~Why avoid one history claim for every African place?~Places experienced different events and timings~All countries share every event~Borders have never changed~A continent contains many histories; causal links need local evidence rather than sweeping claims.~Locate an event|Check its source and date|Assess a geographical consequence
Practice sources describe a coastal trading city, a highland farming district and a desert settlement. They show different environments and livelihoods within Africa.~What do the sources demonstrate?~Variation between places~One environment everywhere~No cities in Africa~Which statement is best supported?~These three places have contrasting environments~Every African resident farms~One desert represents the entire continent~Use named or described places and source details instead of treating a diverse continent as uniform.~Read contrasting sources|Compare specific features|Write a precise claim
Kenya borders Tanzania and Uganda; Tanzania also borders Uganda. Kenya and Tanzania have Indian Ocean coasts; Uganda is landlocked.~Which country in this set is landlocked?~Uganda~Kenya~Tanzania~Which overland route follows shared borders?~Kenya → Uganda → Tanzania~Kenya → Brazil → Uganda~Tanzania → Japan → Kenya~Check each neighbouring pair; a proposed route must connect real borders or explicitly use sea or air travel.~Kenya|Uganda|Tanzania
Fictional population map: a river corridor has dense settlement; an arid interior has sparse settlement. A mining town is an exception within the dry area.~Which area has the denser pattern?~River corridor~Arid interior outside the town~Every place equally~Why is the mining town important to the explanation?~Jobs can attract settlement despite a dry environment~It proves water never matters~It means the entire desert is dense~Physical conditions interact with employment, transport and history; one factor rarely explains every location.~Describe the pattern|Suggest several factors|Investigate an exception
Africa includes the Sahara in the north, the Congo Basin near the Equator and the East African Rift. Rivers, mountains and deserts affect movement and human activity.~Which feature is a large northern desert?~Sahara~Congo rainforest~Lake Victoria~Why can a major river be a connection as well as a barrier?~It may support travel while crossings constrain routes~Rivers never affect travel~Every river has a bridge everywhere~The same feature can enable and restrict movement depending on transport, crossings and local conditions.~Locate a feature|Identify opportunities|Identify constraints
Kenya lies in East Africa, is crossed by the Equator and has an Indian Ocean coast. Its neighbours include Tanzania, Uganda, South Sudan, Ethiopia and Somalia.~Which ocean borders Kenya?~Indian Ocean~Atlantic Ocean~Arctic Ocean~Which arrival briefing is correct?~Kenya has coastal and inland connections~Kenya is landlocked~Kenya lies entirely in Europe~A coastline and neighbouring countries provide different routes for travel and trade.~Locate Kenya|Check its neighbours|Locate the coast
Kenya includes highlands, the Rift Valley, lakes and an Indian Ocean coast. Altitude and access to water influence opportunities, but do not determine every activity.~Which feature describes a long tectonic depression?~Rift valley~River delta~Sand spit~Why might activities vary between highlands and coast?~Altitude, conditions and access differ~All places have identical conditions~Only country borders determine activity~Landscape differences affect opportunities alongside infrastructure, skills, markets and choices.~Locate the landscape|Compare conditions|Connect to human activities
Fictional Kenyan stations: Highland H at 1800 m averages 18°C; Coast C near sea level averages 27°C over the same period. Rainfall varies seasonally and spatially.~Which station is cooler in this example?~Highland H~Coast C~They are equal~Which factor helps explain the temperature contrast?~Higher altitude at H~H is on the Moon~All equatorial places have identical temperatures~Elevation influences temperature; latitude alone does not determine every local climate.~Read graph units|Compare the two stations|Use location to explain differences
Kenya became independent in 1963 and a republic in 1964. Historical transport and settlement decisions can influence later geography, but each causal claim needs evidence.~Which came first?~Independence in 1963~Republic in 1964~They occurred in reverse order~What would help test a railway's influence on settlement?~Dated maps and settlement records~A guess without dates~A single unrelated rainfall reading~Comparing sources before and after a change can test a geographical link without assuming it is the only cause.~Independence in 1963|Republic in 1964|Later changes investigated with evidence
Fictional Kenyan districts: a well-connected highland district is densely settled; a dry remote district is sparse; a transport town grows within the dry district.~What is the exception to a simple dry-equals-sparse rule?~The growing transport town~The sparse remote area~The highland district~Which explanation considers the exception?~Transport jobs and services can attract people~Climate has no influence anywhere~Every dry area has the same population~Use environmental and human factors together, and test them against exceptions.~Describe the distribution|Propose factors|Test the exception
Practice Nairobi neighbourhood sources, not citywide statistics: Area A has a nearby clinic and frequent buses; Area B has long journeys to both. Residents identify access as a priority.~Which issue is directly supported?~Unequal access to services in these examples~Every resident has no services~All neighbourhoods are identical~Which evidence would help choose a new clinic location?~Population needs and travel-time mapping~Only the colour of buildings~A single tourist review~Service planning should consider people and accessibility, and cannot generalise two examples to the entire city.~Map demand|Map existing access|Prioritise unmet needs
A fictional tea journey involves growing leaves, processing and packing, transporting boxes, then selling them. These stages connect primary, secondary and tertiary activities.~Which is a primary activity?~Growing tea leaves~Retailing tea~Transporting boxes~Which stage changes leaves into a packaged product?~Processing and packing~Retail display alone~A passenger ticket sale~Manufacturing changes raw materials; transport and retail provide services along the same chain.~Grow tea|Process and pack|Transport boxes|Sell the product
Fictional flower order: growers harvest, workers grade and cool stems, transport carries them to an airport, and buyers distribute them. Delays can affect a perishable product.~Why use cooling in this model?~To help preserve flower quality~To turn flowers into metal~To remove every transport cost~A flight is delayed. Which response addresses the immediate risk?~Maintain cooling and investigate another shipment route~Leave stems in the sun~Assume flowers cannot deteriorate~A perishable supply chain needs coordinated storage and transport; alternatives have costs and limits.~Harvest|Grade and cool|Transport to airport|Ship to buyer
Fictional safari plan: wildlife needs space; visitors want sightings; local residents seek livelihoods. A wet track is vulnerable to erosion and vehicle damage.~Which choice reduces disturbance?~Keep a suitable distance from wildlife~Surround every animal with vehicles~Leave the designated route everywhere~Which plan considers more than visitors alone?~Use durable routes and include local benefits~Ignore residents and habitat~Guarantee that every tour has no impact~Sustainable decisions balance benefits and impacts; even a careful plan has limitations to monitor.~Identify stakeholders|Compare impacts|Choose and review a plan
Fictional development indicators: District A has higher income but longer clinic travel times than District B. Neither average describes every household.~What does a district average hide?~Differences within the district~The existence of the district~Every comparison between places~Which judgement is most balanced?~Compare several indicators and acknowledge uneven experiences~Income alone answers every development question~One household represents everyone~Development has several dimensions; comparable indicators and their distribution matter.~Choose comparable indicators|Compare results|State a balanced judgement
`;
const GEO_BANK=GEO_BANK_TEXT.trim().split('\n').map(line=>{const v=line.split('~');if(v.length!==11)throw Error('Geography data row: '+v.length);return {source:v[0],recognise:{prompt:v[1],answer:v[2],options:v.slice(3,5)},apply:{prompt:v[5],answer:v[6],options:v.slice(7,9)},reason:v[9],sequence:v[10]};});
if(GEO_BANK.length!==58)throw Error('Expected 58 Geography topics');
